# XSH clearerr()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.clearerr.001
anchor     functions/clearerr.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       clearerr() clears both indicators: on a file stream read to
           end-of-file, feof() becomes 0; on a stream from fdopen() of an
           O_NONBLOCK pipe's read end that has both indicators set (fgetc()
           on the empty pipe failed with EAGAIN, then returned EOF after the
           writer closed), feof() and ferror() both become 0.
note       fgetc() reads while the error indicator alone is set, so the
           second stream gets both; if it does not, the test is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/001-clears-both.c

assertion  XSH.clearerr.002
anchor     functions/clearerr.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With errno set to ERANGE, clearerr() on a stream with its
           end-of-file indicator set, and on one with no indicator set,
           leaves errno ERANGE.
note       Added by POSIX.1-2008 TC1 (Austin Group defect 401).
test       t/002-errno-kept.c

assertion  XSH.clearerr.003
anchor     functions/clearerr.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       On a stream that has read the 1-byte file "a" to end-of-file,
           after "b" is appended through another open() of the file and
           fgetc() has again returned EOF (a precondition: the indicator is
           set; UNRESOLVED if not), fgetc() after clearerr() returns 'b'.
note       fgetc() returns EOF while the indicator is set and reads the
           next byte once it is clear. The other open() is a separate open
           file description, so XSH 2.5.1 does not apply.
test       t/003-reads-again.c
